data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core for .NET 11, the **sync-over-async opt-in for the Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The workaround you had in .NET 10 (`CosmosOptionsExtension` / `EnableSyncOverAsync`) no longer exists.

Every synchronous LINQ operator (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`) and `SaveChanges()` on the Cosmos provider will throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime with no opt-in to suppress it.

### Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Key steps for the migration

1. **Replace all sync LINQ terminals** (`ToList`, `First`, `Single`, `Count`, `Any`, etc.) with their `*Async` counterparts.
2. **Replace `SaveChanges()`** with `SaveChangesAsync()`.
3. **Propagate `async/await` up the call chain** — controllers, services, and anything calling these repositories must also become async.
4. **Remove the old opt-in config** (e.g., `options.EnableSyncOverAsync()`) from your `DbContext` setup — it will cause a compile error since the API no longer exists.

`Add()` / `Remove()` remain synchronous since they only track in-memory state and don't hit Cosmos.
